nodecode-workflow-record is a rewrite of NousResearch's NousResearch/hermes-labs-plugins:

  https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-labs-plugins
  commit 4987918a068fcf4b3efbb584db5dd05e5f57c669
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Nous Research (LICENSE)

The original is a Hermes Agent plugin: a `hermes record` subcommand that attaches
to a Chromium over the Chrome DevTools Protocol, injects an in-page recorder and
writes one JSON file of steps under the Hermes home, plus a bundled skill that
tells /learn how to turn such a recording into a skill that replays it. The
rewrite keeps the recording model -- the same step types, the same
{SECRET:<field name>} masking done both in the page and again from the field's
type and autocomplete hint, the same sorting and rebasing, the same
recordings/<slug>-<stamp>.json file written 0600 in a 0700 directory -- and
reimplements it in Common Lisp against Nodecode's cell contract: the verbs are
the `record` tool and /record, the recorder JS is rewritten for the same three
DOM events, and the skill's guidance is the (help :record) primer, which the
model reads before writing the skill itself. Left out: the `--manual` stdin loop
(a `step` action does the same during a recording), the `--list` flag (the
`list` action and /record list), the BROWSER_CDP_URL and Hermes-config endpoint
precedence (one `cdp_url` setting), and attachment to the browser target with
Target.attachToTarget with flattened sessions (the page target from /json/list
is attached directly; the events and the masking are unchanged). No Puppeteer,
Playwright or npm is involved, here or there: both speak CDP over the socket
directly.
